WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.228 --> 00:00:16.172
For the next three weeks the Canterville ghost fulfilled his ghostly obligations as quietly as possible.

2
00:00:16.852 --> 00:00:22.256
He wore a thick black cloak, tiptoed lightly in his bare feet, and applied the Rising Sun Lubricator

3
00:00:22.296 --> 00:00:23.397
to his chains every night.

4
00:00:24.118 --> 00:00:27.280
Despite all his precautions, he still didn't escape treachery.

5
00:00:27.776 --> 00:00:32.017
He had to be always on the lookout for strings stretched across his path in the dark or

6
00:00:32.057 --> 00:00:34.558
buckets cleverly balanced to spill as he passed.

7
00:00:35.578 --> 00:00:39.940
One night, as he was passing through the library door, he thought he saw someone sitting in one

8
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:40.620
of the chairs.

9
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.221
"Now's my chance," he whispered to himself.

10
00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:48.682
"I'll frighten whoever it is so badly that the whole house will wake with his screams."

11
00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:51.703
And he carefully crept up behind the chair.

12
00:00:52.243 --> 00:00:57.278
He hovered there for a moment, chuckling to himself, and then leapt out with an evil smile and

13
00:00:57.318 --> 00:00:58.498
turned to face his victim.

14
00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:01.961
The Canterville ghost let out a wail and fell back in terror.

15
00:01:02.982 --> 00:01:05.904
Right in front of him, seated motionless, was a horrible sight.

16
00:01:06.484 --> 00:01:09.326
Monstrous hollow eyes peered from a hideous white face.

17
00:01:10.046 --> 00:01:14.389
It wore a loose cloak shredded with age, and in its hand was a gleaming sword.

18
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.272
Never having seen a ghost before, he was naturally very frightened.

19
00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:21.855
He fled all the way back to his room where he hid his face under a sheet.

20
00:01:22.875 --> 00:01:28.980
After a time, when morning was beginning to lighten the sky, the brave Canterville spirit asserted himself, and

21
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.161
he decided to go and speak to the other ghost.

22
00:01:31.942 --> 00:01:34.984
"After all, two ghosts are better than one," he reasoned.

23
00:01:35.784 --> 00:01:39.547
When he returned to the spot, it didn't look as if the other ghost had moved.

24
00:01:40.207 --> 00:01:42.729
He cleared his throat loudly, but the other didn't stir.

25
00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.211
Quietly he reached out to tap his new ally on the shoulder.

26
00:01:46.952 --> 00:01:50.274
The head slipped off and rolled onto the floor, and the bones fell in a heap.

27
00:01:51.434 --> 00:01:56.514
There at his feet was a carved turnip, and on the chair was a pair of broomsticks collapsed

28
00:01:56.574 --> 00:01:57.774
under a moth-eaten curtain.

29
00:01:58.455 --> 00:02:00.716
The sword was nothing but an everyday kitchen knife.

30
00:02:01.136 --> 00:02:01.876
He had been tricked!

31
00:02:02.797 --> 00:02:07.980
Feverish with rage, the ghost raised his withered hands above his head, and using the words of an

32
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:14.083
ancient oath, he swore that when the rooster had crowed three times, deeds of bloodshed and murder would

33
00:02:14.123 --> 00:02:15.244
descend on the castle.

34
00:02:15.904 --> 00:02:17.004
No one would be spared!

35
00:02:17.745 --> 00:02:19.706
Nervously he listened for the rooster's cry.

36
00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:23.348
Morning had arrived, and the family would soon be coming down the stairs.

37
00:02:23.956 --> 00:02:25.917
He waited, but the rooster never crowed.

38
00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:29.779
The ghost was puzzled; this curse had never failed him before.

39
00:02:30.639 --> 00:02:35.501
At last, worried that he might be discovered, he gave up his vigil and retreated to his room.

40
00:02:36.321 --> 00:02:41.183
There he lay down on his low cot feeling more dejected than he ever had before in three

41
00:02:41.263 --> 00:02:41.924
hundred years.

42
00:02:42.764 --> 00:02:46.626
After this, the ghost was not seen again on any nocturnal expedition.

43
00:02:47.346 --> 00:02:51.027
At dinner that night, as the Otis family devoured the roast rooster that Mrs.

44
00:02:51.148 --> 00:02:54.433
Umney had prepared, they decided the ghost must have left.

45
00:02:55.794 --> 00:02:58.315
"I think I'll write a letter to Lord Canterville," said Mr.

46
00:02:58.395 --> 00:02:59.936
Otis, "and tell him the good news."

47
00:02:59.956 --> 00:03:03.578
"Perhaps he'd like to come round for a dinner party," said Mrs.

48
00:03:03.678 --> 00:03:04.559
Otis with a smile.

49
00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:10.502
The twins were a little disappointed, but before long, everyone had found something new to absorb their interest.

50
00:03:11.322 --> 00:03:17.586
Washington devoted himself to his studies, the twins explored the grounds playing cowboys and Indians, and Mrs.

51
00:03:17.726 --> 00:03:21.728
Otis invited the best families from around the neighborhood for elaborate parties.

52
00:03:22.432 --> 00:03:27.694
Virginia's attention was drawn to the young Duke of Cheshire, who paid her the most extravagant compliments when

53
00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:29.035
he visited every weekend.

54
00:03:29.055 --> 00:03:34.817
"Your eyes sparkle like sunlight dancing on the summer sea," he told her as they rode home from

55
00:03:34.877 --> 00:03:36.458
Brockley meadows late in the summer.

56
00:03:37.238 --> 00:03:40.319
Virginia ignored his elaborate flattery and changed the subject.

57
00:03:40.339 --> 00:03:43.621
"You know, I think I miss the ghost, now that he's gone."

58
00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:46.162
"Oh, I'm sure that you don't mean that.

59
00:03:46.522 --> 00:03:48.783
My great uncle met the ghost at the castle once.

60
00:03:49.383 --> 00:03:53.856
He was found shivering on the floor the next morning mumbling ‘chill of death' over and over.

61
00:03:54.497 --> 00:03:56.397
He never said a sane word after that."

62
00:03:57.157 --> 00:03:58.978
Virginia didn't know how to reply.

63
00:03:59.378 --> 00:04:05.260
She thought these stories involving the Canterville ghost were greatly exaggerated, but she also knew that the young

64
00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.700
duke had an honest heart and wasn't in the habit of making up stories.

65
00:04:09.401 --> 00:04:11.921
They passed an encampment of Gypsies, who Mr.

66
00:04:12.021 --> 00:04:16.723
Otis had kindly let stay on the castle grounds, and Virginia suggested a shortcut.

67
00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:21.724
As their horses slipped through a gap in the hedges, her skirt caught on a branch and tore.

68
00:04:22.524 --> 00:04:25.485
"Oh my," said Virginia blushing, "please excuse me."

69
00:04:26.005 --> 00:04:29.226
She quickly rode ahead and went up to her room by the back staircase.

70
00:04:30.106 --> 00:04:34.267
As she was running past the tapestry chamber, she fancied she saw someone inside.

71
00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:35.808
Thinking it was Mrs.

72
00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:38.829
Umney, she looked in to ask her to mend her skirt.

73
00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:41.990
To her immense surprise, it was the Canterville ghost!

74
00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.891
He was sitting by the window watching the yellow leaves fly through the air like strips of autumn

75
00:04:46.931 --> 00:04:52.849
gold. His head was leaning on his hand, and his whole attitude suggested extreme depression.

76
00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:59.312
Virginia's first instinct was to run away, but the ghost looked so melancholy that she was overcome with

77
00:04:59.372 --> 00:05:04.754
pity. "I'm so sorry for you," she said, "but my brothers are going back to school next week,

78
00:05:05.274 --> 00:05:08.275
and then, if you behave yourself, no one will annoy you."

79
00:05:08.295 --> 00:05:14.058
"It is absurd asking me to behave myself," he answered, turning around in astonishment.

80
00:05:14.078 --> 00:05:19.280
"I have to rattle my chains and groan through keyholes; it's my only reason for existing."

81
00:05:20.392 --> 00:05:24.115
"It's no reason for existing at all, and you know you have been very wicked.

82
00:05:24.855 --> 00:05:29.178
It was you who stole my paints in order to keep up that ridiculous stain in the library.

83
00:05:29.979 --> 00:05:34.542
First you took all my reds and purples, and after you took the emerald green, I could paint

84
00:05:34.602 --> 00:05:36.684
nothing but boring pictures in black and white.

85
00:05:37.264 --> 00:05:38.125
Besides, Mrs.

86
00:05:38.245 --> 00:05:40.066
Umney told us that you killed your wife!"

87
00:05:40.967 --> 00:05:42.928
"Well, I admit it," said the ghost.

88
00:05:43.228 --> 00:05:47.411
"However, my wife is long dead now, and I don't think it was very nice of her brothers

89
00:05:47.471 --> 00:05:48.452
to starve me to death!"

90
00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:50.481
"Starve you to death!"

91
00:05:50.881 --> 00:05:51.721
exclaimed Virginia.

92
00:05:51.741 --> 00:05:52.962
"Oh, Mr.

93
00:05:53.042 --> 00:05:56.364
GhostㅡI mean, Sir Simonㅡare you hungry?

94
00:05:57.064 --> 00:05:58.325
I have a sandwich in my room.

95
00:05:58.585 --> 00:05:59.225
Would you like it?"

96
00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:02.127
"No, thank you, I never eat anything now.

97
00:06:02.527 --> 00:06:04.368
I am so lonely and so unhappy.

98
00:06:05.088 --> 00:06:10.631
I haven't slept for three hundred years," he said, and a solitary tear fell from his gray cheek.

99
00:06:11.432 --> 00:06:15.674
Trembling a little, Virginia knelt beside him and looked up into his old, withered face.

100
00:06:15.694 --> 00:06:18.896
"Poor ghost, is there no place where you can sleep?"

101
00:06:19.744 --> 00:06:25.547
He answered in a low, dreamy voice: "Far away beyond the pinewoods there is a little garden where

102
00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:26.868
the hemlock flowers grow.

103
00:06:27.589 --> 00:06:31.551
The nightingale sings, and the cold, crystal moon looks down over the peaceful sleepers."

104
00:06:31.571 --> 00:06:35.073
"You mean the Garden of Death," she whispered solemnly.

105
00:06:35.093 --> 00:06:37.074
"Yes, death.

106
00:06:37.414 --> 00:06:38.455
How I long for it!

107
00:06:39.075 --> 00:06:42.097
To lie in the brown earth with no yesterday and no tomorrow.

108
00:06:42.637 --> 00:06:44.558
To forget time and to sleep at peace."

109
00:06:45.219 --> 00:06:45.739
He paused.

110
00:06:45.759 --> 00:06:47.460
"You can help me, Virginia."

111
00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.926
As the ghost said her name, a cold shudder ran through Virginia, and she looked around the room.

112
00:06:53.567 --> 00:06:58.192
She thought she saw the animals and huntsmen pictured in the tapestries hanging on the walls come to

113
00:06:58.252 --> 00:07:01.336
life. "Beware, little Virginia," they called to her.

114
00:07:01.356 --> 00:07:02.457
"Beware!"
